Venetium 0020 - crashpad on Windows ARM32: CaptureContext in armasm, and the snapshot/util port.

BATCH-ARM-1 section 3D, his decisions 1 and 6 (ARM-1-DECISIONS.md: keep crash reporting on ARM; the assembler split by file type).
- util/misc/capture_context_win_arm.asm (new): CaptureContext for the ARM32 CONTEXT, armasm syntax, byte-identical to the file checked on
  the server before (s3/toolchain/armasm, sha256 0e4955e1...). Venetium 0016's arm asm tool is clang-cl (GAS syntax), which cannot read
  armasm syntax, so the file gets its own build step: util/misc/run_armasm.py (new) runs MSVC 14.44's armasm.exe (/nologo /Fo /I only;
  it rejects /c, /D, /safeseh) with environment.arm, zeroes the object's timestamp, and its .obj is added to crashpad util's sources for
  current_cpu == "arm" (util/BUILD.gn). The x86 MASM files (capture_context_win.asm, safe_terminate_process.asm) are no longer handed to
  the arm asm tool.
- The Windows ARM32 snapshot/util port (~70 lines; CPUContextARM already exists upstream): util/win/context_wrappers.h,
  util/net/http_transport_win.cc, snapshot/win/{cpu_context,exception_snapshot,process_snapshot,system_snapshot,thread_snapshot}_win.*,
  which stopped on "#error Unsupported Windows Arch" / "Port" for ARM32; plus three test-support files so crashpad's own tests compile.
Checked (RUNTIME, real arm-rt21 graph after gn gen): the armasm action runs and gives an ARMNT object defining
crashpad::CaptureContext(CONTEXT*); the 8 previously failing TUs and crashpad_client_win / the handler's exception handler compile as
ARMNT. Device-only: the captured Pc keeps the Thumb bit (as the arm64 sibling stores LR), minidump content, the handler at run time.

